}

{
  "step_1": "The decision reason is based on skewness, specifically preferring the lottery that is more left-skewed (i.e., has a higher concentration of probability mass on favorable outcomes). To evaluate skewness, one must consider both the probabilities and the magnitudes of outcomes. For Lottery A: there is a 25% chance of 6000 (favorable) and 75% chance of 0 (unfavorable). For Lottery B: 25% chance of 4000, 25% chance of 2000, and 50% chance of 0. The total probability of a non-zero outcome is 50% in B versus 25% in A, but the maximum outcome is higher in A. Skewness analysis would require assessing how probability mass is distributed across high vs. low outcomes. In this context, Lottery B spreads its non-zero outcomes across two moderate values (4000 and 2000), each with 25% chance, while A concentrates its 25% chance on a single high value. This makes A potentially right-skewed (a small chance of a very high outcome), whereas B has more mass on intermediate outcomes, possibly making it more left-skewed. Thus, the reason can be applied to the decision problem using the outcome-probability structure.",
